From: Cursor Agent <cursoragent@cursor.com>
Date: Tue, 13 Oct 2026 04:07:24 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 10/14] docs: document outbox events, the audit API and their
 configuration

Resource writes now produce outbox events that the application
publishes, and API writes produce audit records that tenants can list,
so operators need to know what is stored, how it is delivered and how
to recover it.

The CHANGELOG lists the new behaviour, the breaking changes to the
Outbox Python API and the fixed defects. The entries include the update
path: update_spec, which PUT and PATCH use, writes an event, and API
updates write an audit record with action "update". They also say how
the instance check and the reclaim of orphaned allocations fit in: the
status write that turns a service whose instance is gone into Degraded
emits an event with a generated correlation id and no audit record,
allocation writes emit no event, and the dispatcher runs from before
deployment recovery until the controller, the workflow runtime and the
launcher have stopped. There is no separate entry for DELETE reading
X-Correlation-Id: the entry about echoing the correlation id and the
event and audit entries cover it.

The README documents the three FORGEMESH_EVENT_WEBHOOK_* variables and
how events and audit records behave at runtime, and lists
GET /api/v1/audit-events in the REST API table. The module mapping adds
events.py, publishers.py, pagetokens.py and version.py and the new test
files, and moves audit records and outbox events into the registry
database. The backup runbook, which already covers the instance record
file of the process launchers, lists the two new tables and
registry_meta, which holds the page token key that both lists now use,
explains what a restore means for events and audit records (including
the status events written when services are redeployed after the
restart), and shows how to list and redrive dead events.
